<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Click Beattle Hopper | Adi Kinhikar</title><meta name="description" content="Portfolio of Adi Kinhikar, mechanical engineering student at Olin College building toward surgical robotics."><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"><script>document.documentElement.classList.add("js")</script></head><body><nav><div class="wrap"><a class="logo" href="index.html">Adi Kinhikar</a><div><a class="l" href="index.html">Home</a><a class="l  on" href="projects.html">Projects</a><a class="l" href="contact.html">Contact</a></div></div></nav><main><div class="wrap"><div class="page-head"><a class="back" href="projects.html">&larr; All projects</a><p class="eyebrow">Bio-inspired design &middot; Sept 2026&ndash;</p><h1>Click Beattle Hopper</h1><p class="lead">A hopper inspired by click beetles and froghoppers, built from easily replaceable modules.</p></div><div class="facts"><div><span>Inspiration</span><b>Click beetle, froghopper</b></div><div><span>Tools</span><b>SolidWorks, laser cutting</b></div><div><span>Materials</span><b>Fiberwood, rubber tubing, dowels</b></div></div><section><div class="prose"><h2>Inspiration</h2><p>I studied click beetles and froghoppers and the biological components that let them jump, then turned those ideas into a mechanism.</p>
<h2>How it works</h2><p>Bent rubber tubing stores energy, and a wooden dowel inside the tubing acts as a friction-delay trigger.</p>
<h2>Built to be reused</h2><p>Parts connect with pegs and pins, with no hot glue, so any module can be swapped out or reused. That keeps the design sustainable.</p>
<h2>Design process</h2><ol><li>Studied click beetles and froghoppers</li><li>Sketched hopper ideas</li><li>Built a cardboard-and-toothpick sketch model</li><li>Modeled in SolidWorks and laser cut the parts</li><li>Built a first prototype and wrote a change list</li><li>Updated the CAD, re-cut, and built the final version</li></ol>
<p>After every iteration I documented jump height, deformation, and ground contact.</p><div class="chips"><span class="chip">SolidWorks</span><span class="chip">Laser cutting</span><span class="chip">Biomimicry</span><span class="chip">Prototyping</span></div></div></section><section><div class="pn"><a href="project-cvt-guard.html">&larr; CVT Guard</a><a href="project-phb-research.html">PHB-Degrading Microbial Community &rarr;</a></div></section></div></main><footer><div class="wrap">&copy; <span class="yr"></span> Adi Kinhikar &middot; Olin College of Engineering</div></footer><script src="script.js"></script></body></html>
